## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design offline-first, distributed data synchronization with real-world constraints (latency, privacy, scale) in a mobile fleet context. Emphasizes secure, conflict-aware replication without centralized bottlenecks.

## Key Concepts
- WebRTC DataChannels for direct device sync
- CRDT-based namespaces for idempotent merges
- Vector clocks for causality, tombstones for deletes
- End-to-end encryption and minimal key management
- Garbage collection strategy for tombstones and stale data

## Code Example
```javascript
// useP2PQueue.ts
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';
import { P2PSyncManager } from './syncManager';

export const useP2PQueue = () => {
  const [pendingOps, setPendingOps] = useState([]);
  const [isConnected, setIsConnected] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    const sync = new P2PSyncManager();
    sync.onConnectionChange(setIsConnected);
    sync.onPendingOpsChange(setPendingOps);
    return () => sync.disconnect();
  }, []);

  const enqueue = async (namespace, operation) => {
    await syncManager.enqueueOperation(namespace, operation);
  };

  return { pendingOps, isConnected, enqueue };
};
```

## Trade-offs
- **Latency**: Direct P2P reduces round-trip time vs cloud relay
- **Privacy**: Data stays on-device, no third-party exposure
- **Scalability**: Mesh topology scales linearly but requires connection management
